00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.030 align:start position:0%
 
hello natalie huge congratulations  thank you thank you so much i'm so happy  to be talking to you  all right i absolutely loved the movie  and i needed it right now  i wanted to go back to getting the green  light for this one because i know that  the blacklist wound up being  a big deal for you as a writer but i  don't know was there any particular  person or  thing that happened that made going into  production with this idea  a reality for you um such a good  question  you know as you said this script was on  the blacklist nearly 10 years ago  i wrote it when i was like in my mid-20s  a struggling writer like  trying to make it in la and it opened a  lot of doors for me and it came  really close a few times but it never  quite like  came over that hump and i think  sometimes for  you know your readers who are and  viewers who are you know uh  screenwriters you know  that like sometimes a script is like an  ex-boyfriend or an ex  you just have to like put it away and  move on and i had done that  and then about five years ago when i was  pregnant with my first child  no trace camping who's the producer  financier of this film  approached me and they were like you  know we really want to make this movie  we love this script  we need you to rewrite it quick rewrite  which any writer can tell you is an  oxymoron  um and you know just do the work and  like let's go ahead and it was like an  ex begging me to get back together i was  like  okay it's not gonna be different this  time but i'll humor you  and i did it and i dove in and we had a  lot of conversations about what i  thought the movie looked like and felt  like  and you know when i was done they kind  of said why don't you direct it  and i was like what do i have to do do i  have to like make a real or act it out  for you what do you need me to do  and they said no you know exactly what  it is just  say yes like you can do it and um  that's so rare for a female director in  hollywood  i said um okay well thank you for  letting me know what it feels like to be  a straight white guy  i accept and i will do it maybe my next  movies like jurassic park tbd  um and i just dove in and it's been  honestly the greatest experience of my  life it's been so much fun  doing as a jurassic park obsessed  individual i am fully behind the idea of  you doing another jurassic world movie  okay let's let's put that into the world  let's put that into the universe  that's what these interviews are for  aren't they get it out there  yeah so this is this uh was something  that you wrote a long time ago  between i don't know just your growth as  a person  between making the idea more modern  incorporating technology we're doing now  what would you say is the biggest  difference between that original version  and what we see on screen now  some of the things that stayed the same  was really like lucy's essence she kind  of was an extension of me  in it's her own way you know in my 20s  and so i think  she's a character who asks the you know  the world to love her  not despite the fact that she is weird  but because  she is weird which i think is like the  most important  thing for young women to know that like  that's  how hopefully love works um  and then of course you know i like i  i've kind of said this before  anytime someone asks a writer to read  something that they wrote like  ten five six years ago like i'd rather  be buried alive than have to go back  and look at that but writing is a muscle  and you know you kind of learn a ton  about  story and pacing and plot so some of the  kind of mechanics  of the story really changed as you  mentioned technology and  you know um kind of the the pacing and  things like that  but the soul of the story always stayed  the same  i also really wanted to ask you about  working with your casting directors here  because your lead  it's not even just your leads that are  good you have smaller characters  that get their time in the spotlight and  really make the most  of it so what is the key for you to  having a good collaboration with your  casting team  we had such a great casting team and we  really had a casting team  that embraced kind of like thinking  outside of the  usual box and the usual like  you know this is you know this person  should be this way or this person should  be that way we really  kind of tried to kind of find these  little gems  to sort of put in our film and i think  that  one of the great things with casting  directors not just them  alerting you to a read for a particular  part but also  being able to my casting directors were  really showing me  a lot of like you know where my actors  had appeared  in other roles and i think that's so  informative for a director i think  most of my choices weren't necessarily  based on a read and were much more based  on like  where i had seen these actors before and  where i had seen them shine  um and they all you know without  exception  gave me the performance of a lifetime in  this film so i'm  very very grateful to all of them i  cheated and i read that you mentioned  brittany runs a marathon in another  interview and that was my favorite movie  of last year so if that  if that paved the way to this for  woodkarsh i'm really rooting for him  yes it definitely did that paved the way  for lukash blockers paved the way for  geraldine  um for molly snl for ego broad city for  arturo  like i could go on you know you named a  lot of very good ones there  um speaking of molly i'm a big genre fan  horror genre fan i have to ask what  inspired the choice to make her  character obsessed with death um you  know  i think that actually comes from my own  girlfriends i have to be honest  like my favorite murder is one of i  think like the best  podcasts around um and uh  it's just something that like i have a  friend that this character is  sort of loosely based on and her way of  sh we joke that her way of showing  affection is through violence  um so when when molly kind of says i  love you so much i'm gonna push you down  the stairs that is something that has  been said to me  and um i take it with all the love that  it's intended  it's a real art to be able to express  that and have it come across that way  but she pulls it off  she manages that another one of my  absolute favorite parts of the movie  is just the group of friends lucy amanda  and nadine i i really could watch an  entire series of just them  living their life in the city so is all  of that i guess the banter and comedic  timing going back and forth between them  is all of that  scripted and planned or is some of that  naturally coming up on set  as a director one of the best things you  can do  is i i say this all the time a movie is  directed in prep  and by that i mean that like we really  worked those scenes  and we really made sure that they they  worked and they were funny and like they  went back and forth and that dynamic  was rich and natural but  because i had such strong comedians  once you kind of nail the way that it is  on the page you really have an  opportunity  even in a small budget movie like this  to play a little bit and because we  prepared so well  i was able to kind of like let them run  wild and so  the answer to your question is it's kind  of a hybrid it's partially  what was on the page and then partially  just like  these incredible actresses improvising  and adding their own zingers to this  which kind of took it to i think the  next level  the three of them are great i know the  movie is kind of about  letting things go but did you keep any  memorabilia from the production  oh my god well i kept you know  i have a ton of photos from that time  and geraldine actually made me this  really beautiful scrapbook which i have  kept so like i have some confetti that  we  um you know through in the last scene  and a lot of polaroids and then one of  the things that i really  um i'm going to make hopefully someone  lets me do this again a tradition  on my sets is that my first day um first  couple days  on directing this movie i um  took uh photos of a polaroid of every  single person on the crew  and had their name and their role and  that was a way for me to learn  everyone's name but it's also kind of  like a yearbook from the movie and so  i think uh that's kind of gonna be my  ongoing sentimentalist  tradition moving forward i can't even  tell you how much i love that idea  i want to see everyone do it oh i hate  to let you go but i gotta leave  such a pleasure talking to you you too  thanks so much congratulations again  thanks

